condition_name: Pneumonia
condition_slug: pneumonia
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "An inflammation of the lung parenchyma Various bacterial species, fungi and viruses may cause pneumonia The setting in which infection is acquired could be a predictor of the infecting pathogen Bacterial Pneumonia: is defined as bacterial infection of the lung parenchyma associated with recently developed radiological shadowing which may be segmental, lobar or multi lobar. Types • Community Acquired Pneumonia (CAP) • Hospital Acquired pneumonia (HAP) • Ventilator Associated pneumonia (VAP) • Health care associated Pneumonia (HCAP) • Pneumonia in the immunocompromised • Aspiration pneumonia Common bacteria causing CAP: • Streptococcus pneumonia(Most common cause) • Mycoplasma pneumonia • Legionella pneumophilia • Chlamydia pneumonia • Haemophilus influenza • Staphylococcus aureus • Chlamydia psittaci • Coxiella burnetti • Klebsiella pneumonia • Actinomyces israelli • Haemophilus influenzae • Pseudomonas aeruginosa (usually implicated in nosocomial pneumonia)"
clinical_features[5]:
  - type: Typical pneumonia
    features[4]: "Sudden onset fever, chills and rigors",Cough with purulent sputum production,Pleuritic chest pain,Breathlessness with short inspiratory efforts
  - type: Signs
    features[3]: Fever,Herpes labialis,Tachypnoea
  - type: Signs of lung consolidation
    features[7]: Pleural friction rubs,Chest signs are very helpful depending on the phase of the inflammatory response,Dull percussion,Increased tactile and vocal fremitus,Bronchial breath sounds,Whispering pectoriloquy,Crepitations
  - type: Signs of severity
    features[11]: Confusion,Urea > 7mmol/L,Respiratory rate > 30/min,Systolic BP < 90,Age ≤ 65years,"In Children: inability to feed or drink, cyanosis, alteration in level of consciousness",Score 1 point for any of the above features present,0 or 1- home treatment,2 - Hospital-supervised treatment,3 or more- manage in Hospital as severe pneumonia,4 or 5 – ICU Admission
  - type: Atypical pneumonia
    features[8]: Gradual onset,Dry cough,Prominent extra-pulmonary symptoms,Headache,Sore throat,Fatigue,Myalgia,Chest crackles or rales
investigations[11]: FBC +ESR+ CRP,"Serum Electrolyte, Urea and Creatinine",LFT,Blood Culture,Serology,Cold agglutinins,Arterial blood gases/ SPO2,"Sputum gram stain, M/C/S",Urine pneumococcal and legionella antigen,Chest X-ray,Pleural fluid M/C/S
treatment:
  goals[2]: Eliminate the infection,Return to normal lung function
  non_drug[3]: Oxygen to maintain PaO at or above 8kPa,IV fluids especially in severe cases,Antipyretics
  drug[12]: Co-amoxiclav,Benzyl penicillin,"Macrolide (azithromycin 500 mg stat, then 250 mg daily, or clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for up to 14 days)",Cefuroxime axetil,"Fourth generation Cephalosporins: Cefpodoxime",Respiratory quinolone (levofloxacin),Advanced macrolide+ amoxycillin,Advanced macrolide+amoxycillin+clavulanic acid,IV β lactam + advanced macrolide,IV respiratory quinolones+advanced macrolide,Penicillin G+advanced macrolide,"Consider Pneumocystis jiroveci in HIV patients: Co-trimoxazole"
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[3]: "Co-amoxiclav: Nausea, diarrhoea, skin rashes, contraindicated in penicillin hypersensitive individuals","Cefuroxime: Nausea, vomiting, abdominal discomfort, headaches, rarely antibiotic associated colitis","Macrolides: Similar to those mentioned above but usually milder. Hepatotoxicity and antibiotic associated colitis are quite rare."
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[5]: Acute bronchitis,COPD Exacerbation,Pulmonary embolism/infarction,TB,Pulmonary eosinophilia
complications[10]: Empyema thoracis,Pleural effusion,Lung abscess,Lobar collapse,Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism,Pneumothorax,ARDS,Multi organ failure,"Hepatitis, pericarditis, myocarditis, meningoencephalitis",Pyrexia from drug hypersensitivity
prevention[2]: Pneumococcal vaccine,Haemophilus influenzae vaccine
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null